ranslate to the national ticket"], "rationale": "Rhode Island's D+8 lean and steady Democratic presidential record anchor the race in Democratic territory, and the term-limited incumbent leaves no incumbency advantage on either side. With no polls or expert ratings published, the Lean D rating rests on fundamentals and carries wide uncertainty.", "watch": "The first public polls or expert ratings of the race, or any sign that local Democratic infighting shapes national turnout, could move the rating.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Rhode Island sits about eight points to the left of the country in presidential voting and has reliably backed the Democratic ticket in recent elections. With the term-limited Republican president leaving office, neither party enters with an incumbency edge, and the race begins as an open contest on Democratic-leaning terrain.\n\nThe evidence base is thin: no public polls of the race, no expert ratings, and no market pricing are available yet. Recent state headlines point to some Democratic turbulence, as an incumbent governor lost a primary amid anger over a bridge project, but that contest was local and its bearing on a national ticket is unclear. The forecast leans Democratic on fundamentals alone, with unusually wide uncertainty until direct polling of the race arrives.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8249, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7734, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.7988, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7688, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.8114, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.8082, "margin": 9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.7696, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "